1
00:00:00,534 --> 00:00:02,569
Hi, I'm Ross.

2
00:00:02,569 --> 00:00:04,905
For the past several months
I've been working on a campaign

3
00:00:04,905 --> 00:00:07,774
to stop the destruction of video games.

4
00:00:07,774 --> 00:00:13,213
See, most video games work indefinitely,
and... Yeah, that's pretty much it.

5
00:00:13,213 --> 00:00:15,582
But more and more are being
designed to stop working

6
00:00:15,582 --> 00:00:20,120
the second support ends from the
publisher--a kill switch, essentially.

7
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:22,623
It's a form of planned obsolescence

8
00:00:22,623 --> 00:00:26,260
where publishers are destroying
games they've already sold to you,

9
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:27,934
but keeping your money.

10
00:00:27,934 --> 00:00:31,331
And this is all preventable--some
people don't understand that.

11
00:00:31,331 --> 00:00:34,401
I've compared this before to
studios from a century ago

12
00:00:34,401 --> 00:00:37,437
burning their own films after
they were done showing them

13
00:00:37,437 --> 00:00:39,206
to recover the silver content.

14
00:00:39,206 --> 00:00:42,509
And now most films of
that era are gone forever.

15
00:00:42,509 --> 00:00:46,213
That's very similar to what's
happening to video games today.

16
00:00:46,213 --> 00:00:49,750
It's just a terrible practice
for everyone involved.

17
00:00:49,750 --> 00:00:51,451
Now most of my efforts have gone towards

18
00:00:51,451 --> 00:00:55,055
challenging the legality of this
practice in various countries,

19
00:00:55,055 --> 00:01:00,627
because it's so consumer-hostile , it's
not clear if what's going on is even legal.

20
00:01:00,627 --> 00:01:04,531
That's still ongoing, but
now we have a new option:

21
00:01:04,531 --> 00:01:08,802
proposing new law, and for
the entire European Union.

22
00:01:08,802 --> 00:01:11,872
It took months to get put
together and processed,

23
00:01:11,872 --> 00:01:15,175
but now we have a
European Citizens' Initiative

24
00:01:15,175 --> 00:01:18,578
devoted to halting the
destruction of video games.

25
00:01:18,578 --> 00:01:20,180
You can read it yourself.

26
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:23,450
This would require publishers to leave
games they have sold to customers

27
00:01:23,450 --> 00:01:26,119
in a working state at the time of shutdown,

28
00:01:26,119 --> 00:01:28,789
without requiring any
further connection to them

29
00:01:28,789 --> 00:01:31,758
or affiliated parties for
the games to function.

30
00:01:31,758 --> 00:01:34,227
How they would do this would be up to them.

31
00:01:34,227 --> 00:01:38,699
This would also apply to free-to-play
games that contain microtransactions,

32
00:01:38,699 --> 00:01:42,302
because if you purchased a
microtransaction as a good,

33
00:01:42,302 --> 00:01:46,540
then the game is made unplayable,
well then you just lost your goods.

34
00:01:46,540 --> 00:01:48,942
This would NOT require publishers to give up

35
00:01:48,942 --> 00:01:51,244
any of their intellectual property rights,

36
00:01:51,244 --> 00:01:55,082
give up their source code--though
that one would still be nice,

37
00:01:55,082 --> 00:01:57,517
it wouldn't require endless support.

38
00:01:57,517 --> 00:02:00,654
They can end it any time they
want as far as we're concerned.

39
00:02:00,654 --> 00:02:04,124
They wouldn't need to host servers,
though they would have to either

40
00:02:04,124 --> 00:02:08,261
patch out that requirement or else
allow customers to host them privately;

41
00:02:08,261 --> 00:02:13,467
assume any liability for what customers
do with their copies of games.

42
00:02:13,467 --> 00:02:17,037
And finally nothing about this
initiative would have any impact

43
00:02:17,037 --> 00:02:21,141
on publisher business practices
while the game is being supported.

44
00:02:21,141 --> 00:02:25,946
In other words, publishers can
continue doing whatever they want,

45
00:02:25,946 --> 00:02:27,914
as long as you get to keep your game.

46
00:02:27,914 --> 00:02:33,153
It amazes me when some people say "that's
going too far" in favor of the consumer.

47
00:02:33,153 --> 00:02:36,390
Though to be fair, that's
probably not most Europeans.

48
00:02:36,390 --> 00:02:42,095
The idea here is we're JUST trying to stop
games from being destroyed. That's it.

49
00:02:42,095 --> 00:02:47,134
So we're taking as narrow an approach as
possible that still solves the problem.

50
00:02:47,134 --> 00:02:48,602
If we get enough signatures,

51
00:02:48,602 --> 00:02:52,706
then this goes before the European
Union to be considered as new law.

52
00:02:52,706 --> 00:02:55,308
Now if you're on board with this,
you don't have to keep watching--

53
00:02:55,308 --> 00:02:57,010
you can go sign it right now.

54
00:02:57,010 --> 00:03:01,815
But wait! Before you do, you
have ONE CHANCE to do this right.

55
00:03:01,815 --> 00:03:04,217
It's not hard, but if you make a mistake,

56
00:03:04,217 --> 00:03:07,554
your signature is invalidated
and you don't get another chance.

57
00:03:07,554 --> 00:03:12,292
We've made guides at stopkillinggames.com
on how to do this, just in case.

58
00:03:12,292 --> 00:03:16,963
But is this as simple as people sign
this and it becomes law in the EU?

59
00:03:16,963 --> 00:03:19,399
Uh, yeah, it might be!

60
00:03:19,399 --> 00:03:24,237
Now it's not a guarantee, but I think
if we get the signatures, this passes.

61
00:03:24,237 --> 00:03:27,074
Why do I say that? Well it's a few factors:

62
00:03:27,074 --> 00:03:31,978
Politicians like easy wins, and this
doesn't require them to do much at all.

63
00:03:31,978 --> 00:03:35,082
And unless it involves gambling
or protecting children,

64
00:03:35,082 --> 00:03:38,351
politicians generally don't
care about video games.

65
00:03:38,351 --> 00:03:42,689
So this isn't a big consequence to
them, or most of their constituencies.

66
00:03:42,689 --> 00:03:47,094
EU law is already unclear on
a lot of this practice anyway,

67
00:03:47,094 --> 00:03:50,130
and we would be making it
easy for them to settle it.

68
00:03:50,130 --> 00:03:52,966
It falls in line with
other consumer practices--

69
00:03:52,966 --> 00:03:57,337
you know how when you BUY something,
you're generally allowed to KEEP it?

70
00:03:57,337 --> 00:04:00,607
And finally, this is a diversion
from really serious topics

71
00:04:00,607 --> 00:04:03,076
that politicians would
prefer not to deal with.

72
00:04:03,076 --> 00:04:06,480
This would let them stay
busy AND avoid hard stuff.

73
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:10,183
In fact, I would bet if we can
clear the signature threshold,

74
00:04:10,183 --> 00:04:14,621
this passes into law, and we
solve the problem 90% or more.

75
00:04:14,621 --> 00:04:18,692
I say 90% because this
would only impact the EU,

76
00:04:18,692 --> 00:04:25,031
so if a game was released only in China
or Japan, yeah this wouldn't save those,

77
00:04:25,031 --> 00:04:29,636
but most video games are sold
globally, and the EU is part of that.

78
00:04:29,636 --> 00:04:31,404
Once it's a requirement that companies

79
00:04:31,404 --> 00:04:35,942
have to let you keep the games they
sold to you in a major market like that,

80
00:04:35,942 --> 00:04:40,413
I think it spreads whether
it's the law elsewhere or not.

81
00:04:40,413 --> 00:04:45,385
Now unfortunately what I would NOT
bet on--because it's a huge unknown--

82
00:04:45,385 --> 00:04:49,256
is whether we can pass that
signature threshold to begin with.

83
00:04:49,256 --> 00:04:55,095
In order for this to work, we need one
million signatures across the EU...

84
00:04:55,095 --> 00:05:02,536
though really it's MORE than that because some are
going to be invalidated because... who knows why.

85
00:05:02,536 --> 00:05:08,341
So really we need more like
1.2 or 1.3 million signatures.

86
00:05:08,341 --> 00:05:14,781
Oh, and it gets worse, because you need certain
minimum thresholds per country for it to pass.

87
00:05:14,781 --> 00:05:17,350
So, say somebody signs it in Greece.

88
00:05:17,350 --> 00:05:25,292
Well then we need almost 15,000 MORE Greeks to sign
it, otherwise NONE of the Greek signatures count.
[insert correction to the effect of: "Minor mistake, ALL the signatures count, but only if 7+ countries pass the threshold."]

89
00:05:25,292 --> 00:05:28,895
That rule strikes me as
rather obtuse and awkward.

90
00:05:28,895 --> 00:05:33,066
I almost wonder if the EU took a page
from the United States for that one.

91
00:05:33,066 --> 00:05:35,836
Anyway, that's a lot of signatures.

92
00:05:35,836 --> 00:05:40,874
I might be delusional for
attempting this, I don't know yet.

93
00:05:40,874 --> 00:05:46,680
Now the good news is we have one year
to get them all, so we will have time.

94
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:48,248
Although I'll say right now,

95
00:05:48,248 --> 00:05:52,452
there is no way I can get that many
Europeans just from my audience.

96
00:05:52,452 --> 00:05:58,258
The only way this works is by reaching out
to European YouTubers and streamers and so on

97
00:05:58,258 --> 00:06:03,330
to get on board, and
non-English-speaking ones at that.

98
00:06:03,330 --> 00:06:08,134
Now I'll do my part and promote this the
best I can, but I have limited reach.

99
00:06:08,134 --> 00:06:11,104
In fact since I've started Stop Killing Games,

100
00:06:11,104 --> 00:06:15,742
I want to say everyone I've
reached out to never responded.

101
00:06:15,742 --> 00:06:19,946
So me reaching out doesn't
work--it's a total failure rate.

102
00:06:19,946 --> 00:06:23,016
However there are two things that did work:

103
00:06:23,016 --> 00:06:28,021
One is people reaching out to
ME, since I respond to a lot.

104
00:06:28,021 --> 00:06:31,258
And two is, people just
covering this on their own.

105
00:06:31,258 --> 00:06:34,828
That REALLY worked which
means if you're watching this,

106
00:06:34,828 --> 00:06:38,064
you may be a critical link in all this,

107
00:06:38,064 --> 00:06:42,869
because you can tell other YouTubers,
streamers, et cetera about this initiative,

108
00:06:42,869 --> 00:06:44,604
and maybe they'll pick it up.

109
00:06:44,604 --> 00:06:49,609
And again, it's even better if their
core audience is non-English speaking.

110
00:06:49,609 --> 00:06:53,880
The number of native English speakers
in the EU is relatively small,

111
00:06:53,880 --> 00:06:57,517
so we need to connect the dots in
other languages the best we can.

112
00:06:57,517 --> 00:07:02,155
Now I'm offering myself for interviews,
publicity on this issue, whatever,
[ rosswscott@gmail.com ]

113
00:07:02,155 --> 00:07:06,259
to almost anyone with a
European audience of some size.
[ rosswscott@gmail.com ]

114
00:07:06,259 --> 00:07:10,330
In addition to that, the official
spokesperson and backup spokesperson

115
00:07:10,330 --> 00:07:13,667
for the initiative have
offered to speak on this also.

116
00:07:13,667 --> 00:07:17,938
And they are multilingual, and we
have a few more official organizers

117
00:07:17,938 --> 00:07:20,940
who may be willing to discuss
this in other languages as well.

118
00:07:20,940 --> 00:07:26,046
These are the people officially in
charge. You know, actual Europeans.

119
00:07:26,046 --> 00:07:33,119
Yeah, even though I've...helped, officially
I'm not a part of this--I'm not eligible.

120
00:07:33,119 --> 00:07:36,022
Doesn't matter! Whatever
gets us to the finish line.

121
00:07:36,022 --> 00:07:38,591
And speaking of eligibility,
it's pretty simple:

122
00:07:38,591 --> 00:07:42,495
You need to be a citizen of an EU country,
though you don't have to live there;

123
00:07:42,495 --> 00:07:45,398
and you need to be at least
voting age. That's it.

124
00:07:45,398 --> 00:07:49,869
This also means it's not just YOU who can
sign this, but your friends can sign this,

125
00:07:49,869 --> 00:07:54,107
your family can sign this,
girlfriends, boyfriends can sign this.

126
00:07:54,107 --> 00:07:59,079
They all count. Get everybody, and
tell them to get people to sign too.

127
00:07:59,079 --> 00:08:02,482
And yes, it feels weird that
I'm an ineligible American

128
00:08:02,482 --> 00:08:06,619
campaigning for Europeans to sign
a document, but you know what?

129
00:08:06,619 --> 00:08:11,257
If it passes we all win, so we just
have to go to where the openings are.

130
00:08:11,257 --> 00:08:14,728
Oh, and if your relatives or whoever
don't care about video games,

131
00:08:14,728 --> 00:08:19,933
you can explain to them this is also
about NOT losing basic ownership.

132
00:08:19,933 --> 00:08:21,935
Like if video games were cars,

133
00:08:21,935 --> 00:08:26,139
what's happening now would be like
if I sold you a car at full price,

134
00:08:26,139 --> 00:08:30,543
then later--and I wouldn't tell you
how MUCH later, so it's not a rental--

135
00:08:30,543 --> 00:08:33,713
I then came to your house
and repossessed your car,

136
00:08:33,713 --> 00:08:35,415
but I also kept your money.

137
00:08:35,415 --> 00:08:39,786
That is not good business...or
not for the consumer anyway.

138
00:08:39,786 --> 00:08:43,390
Yeah, I guess taking back what
you've sold is great for businesses.

139
00:08:43,390 --> 00:08:47,527
Maybe that's why the industry is worth
more than movies and music combined.

140
00:08:47,527 --> 00:08:51,064
I get it, video games aren't
that important to most people,

141
00:08:51,064 --> 00:08:54,234
but because of that, they're
the canary in the coal mine.

142
00:08:54,234 --> 00:09:00,073
What about farm equipment?
Education tools? Medical devices?

143
00:09:00,073 --> 00:09:04,644
You do NOT want this practice
spreading to other areas, believe me.

144
00:09:04,644 --> 00:09:08,214
Now one thing I want to say is some of
you have already done the math and realize

145
00:09:08,214 --> 00:09:13,186
we need an average of over 2,700 signatures
a day in order to make this work.

146
00:09:13,186 --> 00:09:16,856
I'll say right now we're not
going to get that many every day.

147
00:09:16,856 --> 00:09:20,193
This stuff happens in waves
as big people pick it up.

148
00:09:20,193 --> 00:09:24,364
Plenty of days will be below par, but
others could be 10 times or higher.

149
00:09:24,364 --> 00:09:26,433
So don't write this off too early.

150
00:09:26,433 --> 00:09:28,401
Don't be that guy I've seen in team games

151
00:09:28,401 --> 00:09:31,337
who gives up at the
slightest sign of resistance

152
00:09:31,337 --> 00:09:34,240
and calls it game over 30
seconds into the match.

153
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:35,742
I hate that crap.

154
00:09:35,742 --> 00:09:37,811
I think we're at a crossroads.

155
00:09:37,811 --> 00:09:39,412
If we can get enough signatures

156
00:09:39,412 --> 00:09:43,082
then I think we end this
and change gaming history.

157
00:09:43,082 --> 00:09:47,420
Or we can't get our act together and
the problem gets worse and worse,

158
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:50,356
then in the future we
really don't own anything,

159
00:09:50,356 --> 00:09:53,960
and we're charged a lot
FOR not owning anything.

160
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:57,163
Now maybe we get lucky and this is redundant

161
00:09:57,163 --> 00:10:00,467
due to all the other legal
challenges we have going,

162
00:10:00,467 --> 00:10:04,904
but either way, this is it
for your chance to save games.

163
00:10:04,904 --> 00:10:08,074
There's not going to be a round two.

164
00:10:08,074 --> 00:10:11,311
If you or someone you know is an EU citizen,

165
00:10:11,311 --> 00:10:15,415
you have the power to end
this just by signing a form.

166
00:10:15,415 --> 00:10:18,318
Though this plan may have a critical weakness,

167
00:10:18,318 --> 00:10:23,223
and that I'm counting on gamers to care
about not seeing their games destroyed.

168
00:10:23,223 --> 00:10:25,792
I hope that's not too big an ask,

169
00:10:25,792 --> 00:10:27,961
because years from now
somebody could be saying,

170
00:10:27,961 --> 00:10:31,130
"Yeah, I had the chance to save gaming,

171
00:10:31,130 --> 00:10:34,667
"but it required me to open up
a whole new tab in my browser,

172
00:10:34,667 --> 00:10:39,639
"and fill in my name, and a
CAPTCHA...oh my God... Ennh...

173
00:10:39,639 --> 00:10:44,577
"So I didn't do it. It was just too
much work. It wasn't worth it..."

174
00:10:44,577 --> 00:10:50,083
If that's the general sentiment among
gamers, then yeah I can't overcome that.

175
00:10:50,083 --> 00:10:53,386
Then I'm Don Quixote here fighting windmills.

176
00:10:53,386 --> 00:10:56,122
I hope that's not the case.

177
00:10:56,122 --> 00:11:00,827
So, uh, yeah go sign it, or
tell somebody European about it!

178
00:11:02,228 --> 00:11:05,131
[music]

179
00:11:05,131 --> 00:11:09,402
[Subtitles by danielsangeo]
[music]

180
00:11:09,402 --> 00:11:11,404
[music]

181
00:11:14,707 --> 00:11:17,277
Oh, and this doesn't HAVE to take a year.

182
00:11:17,277 --> 00:11:20,634
If we get way more signatures
than we need, we can end it early.

183
00:11:20,634 --> 00:11:22,849
So you can speedrun this.

